  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1153 PM CDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nConvection persisted well beyond peak heating on Sunday evening,\nresulting in a few reports of isolated wind damage. The\nenvironment wasn't particularly impressive with regard to \ninstability, but mesoscale lift associated with outflow boundaries\nand increasing low-level moisture were enough to overcome weak \nMLCINH. Most of this activity has now dissipated, but a few \nshowers could persist for another hour or two with minimal \nimpacts.\n\nThe upper-level ridge is currently centered from east TX into the\nnorthern Gulf with northwest flow aloft over the Mid-South. While\n500 mb geopotential heights will begin to build over the Mid-South \non Monday, we'll likely see another bout of diurnal convection, \nmainly across north MS and east-central AR. The NBM again captured\nthis potential poorly and was adjusted to include 20% PoPs. Any \nstorms that develop should be short-lived, pulse cells with the \npotential to produce gusty winds and brief heavy downpours. This \nactivity will likely linger into the early evening but is expected\nto dissipate fairly quickly after sunset given the lack of forcing\naloft. This anomalous ridge will continue to build through\nmidweek, with the ridge anchoring over the Mid-South. Geopotential\nheights at 500 mb are progged to exceed 598 dam by Tuesday\nafternoon. Large-scale subsidence will warm mid-level \ntemperatures and help squash precipitation chances Tuesday into \nthe late week period.\n\nTemperatures will flirt with record values at all four climate\nsites much of the upcoming week, with highs pushing 100 F in some\nareas. In addition, the moist air mass will support heat indices\nat or above 105 F. A Heat Advisory is already in effect area-wide\nboth Monday and Tuesday. It will likely need to be extended into\nWednesday for portions of the Mid-South, but drier air advecting\ninto the region from the southeast is resulting in enough spatial\nuncertainty to preclude an extension at this time. Regardless,\nit's going to remain quite hot through the remainder of the work\nweek.\n\nThe deterministic NBM has backed off on the weekend \"cooling\ntrend\" a bit, with forecast temperatures still well above normal\n(highs in the mid/upper 80s) through Sunday. Ensemble guidance\nremains uncertain regarding the evolution of the synoptic pattern\nby this time frame. Most of the LREF members indicate a weak\ntrough developing over the Lower MS Valley by Sunday into early\nnext week. This results in a lot of uncertainty in the\nprobabilistic temperatures, with the inner quartile (25th-75th\npercentile) maximum temperature at Memphis ranging from the mid\n80s to the mid 90s. We're still a few days out from having a good\nhandle on a potential pattern shift, but we're going to be\ndealing with the heat through at least the rest of this week.",
